# Reference corpora and derived artifacts (~13 GB, all reproducible).
# raw/         -> src/method_evaluation/download_reference_data.py
# parallel/    -> build_parallel_corpus.py, sample_corpus.py
# transliterated/, cache/ -> transliterate_references.py (cache makes runs resumable)
data/reference/raw/
data/reference/parallel/
data/reference/transliterated/
data/reference/cache/

# Exception to the rule above: the Nisansa shard results ARE committed.
# They come from a third-party web endpoint one item at a time, so they take
# several people several sessions to produce - not cheaply regenerable, and
# shared progress is the whole point. Manifest is committed too so teammates
# need no dataset download. ~20 MB for the word corpus, no LFS.
!data/reference/nisansa_shards/
*.lock

# ...but not the sentence-corpus shards. Same provenance, but 275k sentence
# results at ~450 bytes each is ~124 MB, and that corpus is a fixed-seed sample
# that regenerates exactly, so it is reproducible at the cost of one ~70 min run.
data/reference/nisansa_shards/augmented_sentences_sample/

# The measured capability tables for the Nisansa endpoint ARE committed: they
# document two upstream bugs (17 sequences it cannot romanize at all, 12 it
# returns unromanized) and the evaluation depends on them. ~200 KB.
!data/reference/nisansa_endpoint/

# Per-item CER arrays: inputs to the significance tests, regenerated by
# run_evaluation.py. The aggregated results (metrics/significance/plots) are
# committed; these are ~68 MB of intermediates.
results/method_evaluation/per_item/

# Working notes
chat-session.txt
